# pi-superagent settings.
#
# Copy this file to config.env in the state directory (~/.pi/superagent unless PI_SUPERAGENT_STATE says
# otherwise), edit it, and run `superagent restart`. The start script reads it on every start, so
# a restart by hand and a restart by the health check run the same router. A variable already set in the
# environment wins over this file.
#
# One KEY=value per line, taken literally: no quotes, no $VARIABLES, no ~, and no comment after a value.
# Each commented-out line shows a setting's default.

# ---- GitHub, the only way in ----

# The repository to watch. Unset, the router runs with intake switched off.
PI_SUPERAGENT_GITHUB_REPO=your-org/your-repo

# The account the agent replies from, if it has one of its own: its comments are never requests. Leave it
# unset when you and the agent share an account. The agent's replies then carry a hidden marker instead,
# and naming your own account here would discard every comment you write.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_GITHUB_SELF=your-agent-bot

# The agent's own GitHub account: a file holding a token for it (classic, repo scope), read on every call.
# The router then reads the watched repository and posts every reply as that account. Give the account
# access to the watched repository, set PI_SUPERAGENT_GITHUB_SELF to its login, and keep the file readable
# only by you (chmod 600). Workers still act as the account gh is signed in as, and the router reads their
# pull requests as that account too, since it opened them. Unset, the router acts as gh's account.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_GITHUB_TOKEN_FILE=/absolute/path/to/token

# Author patterns that are bots, never people. A * matches anything.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_BOTS=pi-*,fleet-*

# Who the router hears. Unset, only people with write access to the repository: GitHub's OWNER, MEMBER
# (any member of the owning organization) and COLLABORATOR. Everyone else's issues, comments and reviews
# are dropped before the judge sees them, because a request starts a worker with a shell on this machine.
# Set, this comma-separated list of logins replaces that rule, so include your own. A * matches anything.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_ALLOWED=your-login,a-teammate

# ---- the router ----

# Milliseconds between polls of GitHub when nothing else wakes the router.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_TICK_MS=30000

# The panel's port, on 127.0.0.1.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_PANEL_PORT=8788

# The judge's thinking level, in pi's names for them.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_JUDGE_THINKING=low

# Seconds `superagent stop` waits for the router to exit before closing its tab.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_GRACE=30

# ---- herdr ----

# The herdr binary, if it is not on PATH.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_HERDR=herdr

# The workspace that holds the router and its workers.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_HERDR_WORKSPACE=superagent

# The router's agent name in herdr.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_SESSION=superagent

# ---- workers ----

# Workers on a run at once. Workers parked between runs do not count; further tickets wait their turn.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_MAX_WORKERS=4

# An absolute path for the per-ticket workspaces. The default is the work directory in the state directory.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_WORKTREES=/absolute/path/to/workspaces

# By default a new worker clones the watched repository and checks out superagent/<ticket-id>.
# Set this command to replace the default checkout. It runs with PI_SUPERAGENT_TICKET and
# PI_SUPERAGENT_DIR set. It runs again whenever the ticket gets a fresh worker, so make it safe to repeat.
# If it fails, the ticket fails with the command's own output.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_PREPARE=[ -d .git ] || git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/your-org/your-repo .

# Command names a worker may not run, comma-separated. Names only: no arguments, no paths.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_BLOCKED=gh,ssh

# Set to 1 to let workers start subagents, workflows and other sessions. Delegated work escapes the
# ticket's mandate, workspace and report, so it is off by default.
#PI_SUPERAGENT_ALLOW_DELEGATION=1
